@@ -137,6 +137,67 @@ to indicates which is the bigger version:
 * ``1`` if the first version is greater than the second version.
 
 
+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text,
+one version per line, which makes the output easy to consume
+in a shell loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc1
+    1.2.0-rc1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 2.0.0 1.0.0 | while read v; do echo "found $v"; done
+    found 1.0.0
+    found 2.0.0
+    $ printf '2.0.0\n\n1.0.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.0.0
+    2.0.0
+
+If no versions are given at all (no arguments and empty standard in),
+or if any version is not valid, nothing is printed on standard out, an
+error message is printed on standard error, and the return code is 2.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to choose from. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as a JSON string, so it can be
+parsed with ``fromJSON()`` in GitHub Actions::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc1 1.10.0
+    "1.10.0"
+    $ printf '1.0.0\n1.1.0\n' | pysemver latest
+    "1.1.0"
+
+If no versions are given, any version is not valid, or all versions are
+prereleases, nothing is printed on standard out, an error message is
+printed on standard error, and the return code is 2.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
